  "text": "Lung cancer is the most commonly diagnosed cancer (11.6%) in men and women and remains the primary cause of cancer-related deaths worldwide . Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) accounts for about 85% of all lung cancer cases 1. Radiotherapy (RT) plays a significant role in NSCLC treatment. However, radiation resistance and high local failure rate are still the major limitations regarding patient prognosis 2, 3. The development of immune-checkpoint blockade (ICB) treatment makes RT combined with ICB a promising treatment option, but the synergistic effect remains clinically unsatisfactory 4–6. Therefore, finding safe combination strategies to enhance the antitumor efficacy of radiation is an urgent requirement."
}

